   "brief": "The personal, pre-existent Word (John 1:1, 1:14; Revelation 19:13; 1 John 1:1).",
   "context": "Kept as a loanword under the three-part test: it is a title, it occurs in four verses, and the English candidate actively misleads. In current usage 'the Word' commonly means the Bible, so 'the Word was God' gives many readers a false meaning, and 'word' alone conveys nothing of what a Greek-speaking reader heard (reason, the ordering principle of the world) or of God's creating speech in Genesis 1. A note is required at John 1:1 covering both backgrounds.\n\n**The standard view.** Nearly all translations use 'Word'. Most scholars see John's main background as the Hebrew Bible's 'word of the Lord' and Wisdom traditions, which 'Word' reflects and 'Logos' does not; the note must say so.\n\nScholars disagree on this word, and this rendering is an editorial choice.",
